1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,730
You type python app.py, press Enter, and
a few milliseconds later,

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:06,276
your program is running.

3
00:00:06,646 --> 00:00:08,538
But what actually happened in between?

4
00:00:08,908 --> 00:00:11,461
Is Python interpreted, or is it
compiled?

5
00:00:11,831 --> 00:00:15,419
The honest answer is: both. And by the
end of this video,

6
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:16,875
you'll see exactly how.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,503
Most people describe Python as an
interpreted language,

8
00:00:21,523 --> 00:00:25,987
as if something reads your text file
line by line and simply obeys it.

9
00:00:26,357 --> 00:00:27,627
That's not what happens.

10
00:00:27,997 --> 00:00:31,051
In the standard implementation, called
CPython,

11
00:00:31,071 --> 00:00:33,732
your source code is first compiled into
a

12
00:00:33,752 --> 00:00:36,348
simpler instruction set called bytecode.

13
00:00:36,718 --> 00:00:41,293
Then a program called the Python virtual
machine executes that bytecode,

14
00:00:41,313 --> 00:00:42,952
one instruction at a time.

15
00:00:43,322 --> 00:00:48,006
So CPython compiles, and then it
interprets. Two phases, not one.

16
00:00:49,133 --> 00:00:50,930
Here's the full journey of your code.

17
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:54,775
First, the tokenizer breaks your source
text into tokens.

18
00:00:55,145 --> 00:00:57,936
The parser arranges those tokens into a
tree,

19
00:00:57,956 --> 00:00:59,935
called the abstract syntax tree.

20
00:01:00,305 --> 00:01:03,351
The compiler walks that tree and emits
bytecode,

21
00:01:03,371 --> 00:01:05,139
stored inside a code object.

22
00:01:05,509 --> 00:01:08,459
Finally, the virtual machine runs the
bytecode,

23
00:01:08,479 --> 00:01:10,544
and your program produces output.

24
00:01:10,914 --> 00:01:12,532
Let's zoom into each stage.

25
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:16,869
Take a single line: total = price * 2.

26
00:01:17,239 --> 00:01:21,792
The tokenizer splits it into five
tokens: a name, an equals sign,

27
00:01:21,812 --> 00:01:24,255
another name, a star, and a number.

28
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:27,996
The parser then applies the grammar
rules, and builds a tree.

29
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:32,371
At the top is an assignment. On the
right side, a multiplication,

30
00:01:32,391 --> 00:01:36,458
with the name price on the left and the
constant two on the right.

31
00:01:36,828 --> 00:01:40,065
Notice that the tree captures meaning
and structure,

32
00:01:40,085 --> 00:01:43,511
not just text. And syntax errors are
caught right here,

33
00:01:43,531 --> 00:01:45,328
before any of your code runs.

34
00:01:46,467 --> 00:01:49,084
Now the compiler turns that tree into
bytecode.

35
00:01:49,454 --> 00:01:51,997
Here's a tiny function that adds two
numbers.

36
00:01:52,367 --> 00:01:54,633
Using the built-in dis module,

37
00:01:54,653 --> 00:01:57,606
we can see its bytecode in Python 3.12.

38
00:01:57,976 --> 00:02:01,585
LOAD the variable a. Load the variable
b. Add them

39
00:02:01,605 --> 00:02:04,707
with a binary operation. Return the
result.

40
00:02:05,077 --> 00:02:07,567
These instructions live in a code
object,

41
00:02:07,587 --> 00:02:11,426
along with the constants and variable
names the function needs.

42
00:02:11,796 --> 00:02:16,691
And for imported modules, CPython caches
this bytecode in .pyc files,

43
00:02:16,711 --> 00:02:21,749
inside the __pycache__ folder, so the
next import can skip compilation.

44
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:26,686
Now the virtual machine takes over. It's
a stack machine:

45
00:02:26,706 --> 00:02:29,624
instructions push and pop values on a
stack.

46
00:02:29,994 --> 00:02:31,726
Let's call add(2, 3).

47
00:02:32,096 --> 00:02:36,287
LOAD_FAST a pushes two onto the stack.
LOAD_FAST b pushes three.

48
00:02:36,657 --> 00:02:41,880
BINARY_OP + pops both values, adds them,
and pushes the result: five.

49
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:45,783
RETURN_VALUE pops the five, and hands it
back to the caller.

50
00:02:46,153 --> 00:02:49,878
That loop, fetch an instruction, execute
it, move to the next,

51
00:02:49,898 --> 00:02:51,811
is the heart of the interpreter.

52
00:02:52,933 --> 00:02:56,749
But where do values actually live? In
memory, as objects.

53
00:02:57,119 --> 00:02:59,462
When you write x equals a list of one,

54
00:02:59,482 --> 00:03:03,380
two, three, Python doesn't put that list
inside a box called x.

55
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:07,627
It creates a list object in memory, and
binds the name x to it.

56
00:03:07,997 --> 00:03:13,355
Now y = x. No copy is made. Both names
point at the same object.

57
00:03:13,725 --> 00:03:17,093
The object keeps a reference count.
Right now, it's two.

58
00:03:17,463 --> 00:03:20,236
Change it through y, and x sees the
change,

59
00:03:20,256 --> 00:03:24,067
because it's the same object. Reassign y
to something else,

60
00:03:24,087 --> 00:03:26,145
and the count drops back to one.

61
00:03:26,515 --> 00:03:30,648
When the count reaches zero, CPython
frees the object immediately.

62
00:03:31,018 --> 00:03:33,714
Objects that reference each other in
cycles are

63
00:03:33,734 --> 00:03:36,198
cleaned up by a separate garbage
collector.

64
00:03:37,333 --> 00:03:40,032
Functions add one more idea: frames.

65
00:03:40,402 --> 00:03:44,345
Every time you call a function, the
interpreter creates a frame,

66
00:03:44,365 --> 00:03:48,432
holding that call's local variables and
its place in the bytecode.

67
00:03:48,802 --> 00:03:53,582
Calling a function from inside another
function stacks a new frame on top.

68
00:03:53,952 --> 00:03:56,982
When a function returns, its frame is
removed,

69
00:03:57,002 --> 00:03:59,502
and execution continues in the caller.

70
00:03:59,872 --> 00:04:02,612
This is exactly what you see in a
traceback,

71
00:04:02,632 --> 00:04:05,936
and why runaway recursion ends with a
RecursionError.

72
00:04:07,067 --> 00:04:09,840
So why does any of this matter in real
projects?

73
00:04:10,210 --> 00:04:12,416
When code is slower than you expect,

74
00:04:12,436 --> 00:04:16,003
the dis module shows what the
interpreter really executes.

75
00:04:16,372 --> 00:04:19,906
Understanding references explains the
classic bug where

76
00:04:19,926 --> 00:04:22,684
two variables unexpectedly change
together,

77
00:04:22,704 --> 00:04:24,429
like when you share a list.

78
00:04:24,798 --> 00:04:28,746
And the __pycache__ folder explains why
imports are faster the second time.

79
00:04:29,117 --> 00:04:34,806
One last note: everything here describes
CPython. Other implementations,

80
00:04:34,826 --> 00:04:37,977
like PyPy, add just-in-time compilation,

81
00:04:37,997 --> 00:04:42,814
and recent CPython versions optimize hot
bytecode on the fly.

82
00:04:42,834 --> 00:04:46,383
The model stays the same. The details
evolve.

83
00:04:47,533 --> 00:04:48,483
Let's recap.

84
00:04:48,853 --> 00:04:53,761
Python source is tokenized, parsed into
a tree, compiled to bytecode,

85
00:04:53,781 --> 00:04:56,189
and executed by a virtual machine.

86
00:04:56,559 --> 00:04:58,849
Variables are names bound to objects,

87
00:04:58,869 --> 00:05:02,221
and objects live as long as something
references them.

88
00:05:02,591 --> 00:05:05,907
Each function call gets its own frame on
the call stack.

89
00:05:06,277 --> 00:05:07,152
If this helped,

90
00:05:07,172 --> 00:05:09,776
subscribe, and tell me in the comments
which

91
00:05:09,796 --> 00:05:11,684
system you want visualized next.

